 | 79 | A claim's rule is the bytes of its **first** freeze commit. Before any score is cited, `tools/round-38/freeze-drift.py --freeze <first freeze> --rev <measured>` runs over the claim's kill-rule paths (claims doc, counters, carriers, oracles) and must print zero commits. A non-empty list means the claim is scored with the first-freeze bytes and the amendment is reported, never adopted — whatever note calls it "pre-scoring" once any seat has run a counter or produced claim data. | `corpus/lessons/round-38.md` M38-1; `.ufo/waves/round-38/l1/freeze-drift-077e212b.json` (exit 1: `79f69347`, `f1010f67`) vs `freeze-drift-cb4326b2.json` (exit 0) | executes 55 |
 | 80 | Control generators and control receipts land **in** the freeze commit, and a control replays at the freeze revision to the receipt's verdict. A generator fixed after the freeze makes the receipts certify bytes the freeze never held: run `freeze-drift.py` over the generator path too, and treat a drift as a reproducibility defect to disclose, not a silent fix. | `corpus/lessons/round-38.md` M38-2; `.ufo/waves/round-38/w1/R38ReviewerControlReplay.md` F1–F3; `.ufo/waves/round-38/l1/freeze-drift-gen-controls.json` (exit 1: `efd5c47a`) | sharpens 55, 66 |
 | 81 | A pointer-repair claim names the intended document in the freeze, and its check binds the repaired pointer to that path **and** to the document's git object at the base. Path existence alone is not a repair: a stub written at the unresolved path meets an existence-only counter with zero self-assessment edits. Before data, the control suite carries a "materialise the missing path" control; after data, `tools/round-38/intended-doc.py` (or the claim's successor counter) must print `bound`. | `corpus/lessons/round-38.md` M38-6; `.ufo/waves/round-38/w2/R38W2C2Controls.md` §3 (S2/S1 green under the frozen C2 counter); `.ufo/waves/round-38/l1/intended-doc/{landed-0fc61133,base,shortcut-s2}.json` (exit 0 / 1 / 1) | sharpens 77 |
+| 82 | A negative existence claim ("absent", "exists nowhere on this host") names the root of the sweep behind it **and** roots that sweep at the base its cited relative path is relative to (package root, campaign root), or at an ancestor of that base. A sweep of a sibling tree supports nothing about the path; the claim's scope is the swept root, never the host. | `corpus/lessons/round-38-verifier-spotcheck.md` §5 R38-CL1; `corpus/lessons/round-39.md` M39-1. Gate: `tools/round-39/absence-sweep.py --claim <relpath> --root <base>...` — on the round-38 claims a fleet-rooted sweep exits 0 ABSENT (the false green the lesson relied on), adding the shokuknow root exits 1 PRESENT; `--selftest` 18 cases (`.ufo/waves/round-39/l1/absence/summary.txt`) | sharpens 56, 57, 65 |
 
